End of commercials back to show.
广告结束返回放映。

00:37:40.550 --> 00:37:46.869
But here's a thing that we were starting to unpack in a talk, but we said, ah, let's save it for the show.
但这是我们在一次演讲中开始解开的一件事，但我们说，啊，让我们把它留到节目中吧。

00:37:46.869 --> 00:37:55.550
And we can also dip into this with Carter, but it's so fascinating to me that you guys were the age of the characters you were writing essentially.
我们也可以和卡特一起探讨这个问题，但这对我来说太有趣了，因为你们基本上就是你们所写角色的年龄。

00:37:55.550 --> 00:37:56.389
Yeah.
是的。

00:37:56.389 --> 00:38:10.469
And yet it was framed as a flashback from an older wiser man who was offering this kind of wisdom and perspective born of his years of being farther along and being able to look back and contextualize it.
然而，它被认为是一位年长智者的闪回，他提供了这种智慧和观点，这些智慧和观点源于他多年来的走得更远，能够回顾过去并将其置于背景中。

00:38:10.469 --> 00:38:36.559
And so you were having to, from inside of the experience, you were having to almost beam your consciousness forward 20 or 25 years and reflect back on the moment you were in, which is like a heroic amount to ask, I think of anyone much less young kids running their first TV show, were you conscious of the weirdness of that?
所以你不得不，从经历的内部，你不得不几乎把你的意识投射到20或25年之前，并回想你所处的那一刻，这就像一个英雄般的问题，我认为任何人，尤其是小孩子，运行他们的第一个电视节目，你意识到这很奇怪吗？

00:38:36.559 --> 00:38:39.000
It seems weirder now, in a way.
在某种程度上，现在看起来更奇怪了。

00:38:39.000 --> 00:38:40.639
It almost didn't seem that weird at the time.
当时看起来几乎没有那么奇怪。

00:38:40.639 --> 00:38:44.639
And I think it's because we fancied ourselves older than our years.
我认为这是因为我们幻想自己比实际年龄更老。

00:38:44.639 --> 00:38:48.840
I think, yeah, we were like 32 writing 52 or something, right?
我想，是的，我们就像 32 岁写 52 岁之类的，对吧？

00:38:48.840 --> 00:38:55.039
When we were writing, thinking about future Ted's perspectives and his mid-50s, whatever he is, we're now far closer to that.
当我们写作时，思考未来特德的观点和他 50 多岁的人，无论他是什么，我们现在已经更接近那个目标了。

00:38:55.039 --> 00:39:02.760
And I look back at it and I do think it had something to do with wanting, being drawn to the idea of reassurance of the future.
回顾过去，我确实认为这与想要、被对未来放心的想法所吸引有关。

00:39:02.760 --> 00:39:06.599
There's a future you, because you feel so lost when you're 30, right?
未来的你是有的，因为30岁的时候你会感到很失落，对吗？

00:39:06.599 --> 00:39:09.119
You're not actually quite an adult, right?
其实你还没有成年，对吧？

00:39:09.119 --> 00:39:11.239
We were, we all felt like frauds.
我们，我们都觉得自己像个骗子。

00:39:11.239 --> 00:39:13.960
We were all the same age, we were all these little kids.
我们都是同龄人，我们都是小孩子。

00:39:13.960 --> 00:39:17.360
We somehow fooled our way into having a TV show.
我们不知何故搞了一个电视节目。

00:39:17.360 --> 00:39:29.519
And like we, I think we were just imagining our future selves telling us what we wanted to hear now in this moment because we felt lost, because we felt scared, because we felt haunted.
就像我们一样，我认为我们只是想象未来的自己告诉我们现在想听到什么，因为我们感到失落，因为我们感到害怕，因为我们感到困扰。

00:39:29.519 --> 00:39:33.280
And that idea of that future Ted gave us so much confidence.
特德对未来的想法给了我们很大的信心。

00:39:33.280 --> 00:39:36.960
Like it just, it sort of lifted us up to be looking through that lens.
就像它一样，它有点让我们通过这个镜头来观察。

00:39:36.960 --> 00:39:41.199
And by pretending to be that thing, we sort of became that thing.
通过假装成为那个东西，我们就变成了那个东西。

00:39:41.199 --> 00:39:42.719
And that's so great.
那真是太棒了。

00:39:42.719 --> 00:39:46.760
Now we're sort of the age, almost the age of the of future Ted.
现在我们已经到了这个时代，几乎是未来特德的时代。

00:39:46.760 --> 00:39:49.559
And I still feel just about as confused as I ever did about life.
我仍然像以前一样对生活感到困惑。

00:39:49.559 --> 00:39:52.280
But I feel a little older and a little wiser.
但我感觉自己老了一些，也聪明了一些。

00:39:52.280 --> 00:40:03.960
And I think another big part of it was, We were aware, we were, Carden and I, we're big thinkers, we're overthinkers, and we were very aware that a chapter of our lives had ended.
我认为其中的另一个重要部分是，我们意识到，我们，卡登和我，我们是伟大的思想家，我们是过度思考者，我们非常清楚我们生命中的一个章节已经结束了。

00:40:03.960 --> 00:40:05.920
The 20s in New York part had ended.
20 世纪 20 年代的纽约部分已经结束。

00:40:05.920 --> 00:40:18.239
We were writers for David Letterman, and we made this leap, and this will be a very heavy thing to bring up and talking about a sitcom, but we, you know, 9-11 happened a little bit before, like that happened right before we moved to LA.
我们是大卫·莱特曼的编剧，我们实现了这一飞跃，提起并谈论一部情景喜剧将是一件非常沉重的事情，但我们，你知道，9-11 事件发生过一段时间，就像我们搬到洛杉矶之前发生的那样。

00:40:18.239 --> 00:40:23.159
And there was, this is feeling in New York City sort of writ large that something had ended.
在纽约市，有一种明显的感觉，那就是某些事情已经结束了。

00:40:23.159 --> 00:40:27.599
Some era had ended, something had changed that was not gonna change back.
某个时代已经结束，有些事情已经发生了改变，并且不会再变回来。

00:40:27.599 --> 00:40:31.559
And then we found ourselves living in Los Angeles trying to think of a pilot idea to write.
然后我们发现自己住在洛杉矶，试图想出一个写作的试点想法。

00:40:31.559 --> 00:40:37.679
And we wrote about this nostalgia for our 20s in New York when we were like 30 and one day old.
我们写了关于我们 20 多岁时在纽约的怀旧之情，当时我们才 30 岁零一天。

00:40:37.679 --> 00:40:42.760
We didn't, we were nostalgic for our 20s in New York immediately.
但我们没有，我们立即怀念20多岁的纽约时光。

00:40:42.760 --> 00:40:47.519
And but nostalgic for like a time before this horrible tragedy hadn't happened, right?
但怀念这起可怕的悲剧没有发生之前的一段时光，对吧？

00:40:47.519 --> 00:40:58.539
We were still writing for Letterman when 9-11 happened And then we left to move to LA the spring after that, like the spring of 2002.
9·11 事件发生时，我们仍在为莱特曼写作，之后的春天，比如 2002 年的春天，我们离开并搬到了洛杉矶。

00:40:58.539 --> 00:41:20.659
And I just think the sort of grabby tasks and the instant requirement to become more of a grown-up from living through 9-11 in New York and the instant nostalgia and the desire to sort of have things make sense or be back in some simpler time, inhabit a different time in your brain and your memories when things didn't feel the way they felt after 9-11.
我只是认为，在经历了纽约的“9·11”事件之后，那种抓紧的任务、想要变得更加成熟的即时要求，以及瞬间的怀旧和想要让事情变得有意义或回到某个更简单的时间的渴望，在你的大脑和记忆中占据了一个不同的时间，当事情不像9·11之后那样的时候。

00:41:20.659 --> 00:41:24.300
And I think that weirdly really informed the show.
我认为这确实奇怪地告诉了这部剧。

00:41:24.300 --> 00:41:31.579
It made us like maybe a little older than our years, and it made us a little more reflective, a little more nostalgic.
它让我们感觉自己比实际年龄老了一点，也让我们多了一点反思，多了一点怀旧。

00:41:31.579 --> 00:41:39.260
And I think part of that is how we accessed writing from that future perspective, even when we were in our thirties and we're pretend to be the old wise guy.
我认为其中一部分是我们如何从未来的角度来看待写作，即使我们已经三十多岁了，我们假装自己是一个聪明的老家伙。

00:41:39.260 --> 00:41:43.179
But somehow we could sort of fake our way into being that at least on paper.
但不知何故，我们可以假装成为这样的人，至少在纸面上是这样。

00:41:43.179 --> 00:41:50.260
And I sometimes people will quote lines from future to tell me and say that got them through a really hard time.
有时人们会引用未来的台词来告诉我，并说这让他们度过了一段非常艰难的时期。

00:41:50.260 --> 00:41:52.579
Thank you for those wise words and I'm talking.
谢谢你的智慧之言，我正在说话。

00:41:52.579 --> 00:42:00.340
We were like 31 wishing we had that wisdom and sort of like play acting it, but it worked somehow, right?
我们就像 31 岁的人一样希望自己拥有这种智慧，并且有点像演戏一样，但它不知何故奏效了，对吧？

00:42:00.340 --> 00:42:06.940
It feels to me like Kenneth Branagh at 31 playing Lear, and you're like, what are you doing?
对我来说，这感觉就像 31 岁的肯尼思·布拉纳 (Kenneth Branagh) 扮演李尔王 (Lear)，然后你就会想，你在做什么？

00:42:08.420 --> 00:42:10.699
But also it's like he's good at it.
但也好像他很擅长。

00:42:10.699 --> 00:42:11.539
Right.
正确的。

00:42:11.539 --> 00:42:14.380
And he sort of, and does it elevate you?
他有点这样，这会让你提升吗？

00:42:14.380 --> 00:42:16.380
It's sort of that idea of like the fake until you make a thing.
在你制造出东西之前，这有点像假货。

00:42:16.380 --> 00:42:22.019
Like it somehow it elevated us and made us feel like we really were that older, was it?
就像它以某种方式提升了我们，让我们感觉自己真的老了，不是吗？

00:42:22.019 --> 00:42:29.840
You know, speaking of, of Jordan, it seems like where we talk you about a bit, she has a thing which she'll sometimes say to her patients.
你知道，说到乔丹，似乎在我们谈论你的地方，她有时会对她的病人说一些话。

00:42:29.840 --> 00:42:35.480
If someone's really confused, really tied and not about something, she'll say, what is your 85 year old self say?
如果有人真的很困惑，真的很束缚并且不关心某件事，她会说，你85岁的自己会说什么？

00:42:35.480 --> 00:42:36.679
She's gonna like him in.
她会喜欢他的。

00:42:36.679 --> 00:42:37.599
She didn't like the show.
她不喜欢这个节目。

00:42:37.599 --> 00:42:39.239
Yeah, but if it's a great question.
是的，但如果这是一个很好的问题。

00:42:39.239 --> 00:42:42.840
And she said, if you ask yourself, what is my 85 year old self?
她说，如果你问自己，85岁的我是什么样的？

00:42:42.840 --> 00:42:45.500
It all, the answer comes real fast.
一切，答案来得很快。

00:42:45.500 --> 00:42:47.000
And it's real service.
这是真正的服务。

00:42:47.000 --> 00:42:47.760
Yes, yes, yes.
是的，是的，是的。

00:42:47.760 --> 00:43:02.920
And I think, you know, I mentioned this that piece I wrote about Ted for my muse letters, but Dick Schwartz has the thing internal family systems, IFS, which is like really it's called parts work and you're locating these parts of yourself that are sometimes at odds with each other, but they're all in you.
我想，你知道，我在为我的缪斯信件写的关于泰德的那篇文章中提到了这一点，但迪克施瓦茨有内部家庭系统，IFS，这就像真的它被称为部分工作，你正在定位自己的这些部分，有时彼此不一致，但它们都在你身上。

00:43:02.920 --> 00:43:09.599
And it strikes me that you and Carter like, and me too, we have an elder in us.
让我印象深刻的是，你和卡特都喜欢，我也喜欢，我们身上有一位长辈。

00:43:09.599 --> 00:43:10.440
Right.
正确的。

00:43:10.440 --> 00:43:22.744
We have an older, I don't know if it's ancestral or just a wise old, something connected more towards the bigger picture the perennial kind of wisdom.
我们有一个更古老的，我不知道它是祖传的还是只是一个智慧的老人，一些与更大的图景更相关的东西，即永恒的智慧。

00:43:22.744 --> 00:43:24.384
And we can go there.
我们可以去那里。

00:43:24.384 --> 00:43:29.880
We can we can access it at 31 or 30 whenever you guys were writing.
每当你们写作时，我们都可以在 31 或 30 访问它。

00:43:29.880 --> 00:43:39.395
The other thing that I don't know if it was you or Carter, but first season what year was older Ted narrating, 2030?
另一件事情，我不知道是你还是卡特，但第一季是哪一年，泰德旁白是 2030 年？

00:43:39.395 --> 00:43:40.298
2030, yeah.
2030 年，是的。

00:43:40.298 --> 00:43:40.798
2030.
2030年。

00:43:40.798 --> 00:43:57.449
So one of you guys said it's actually actually the most optimistic show in the world because it's saying the world will still be here in 2030, like they'll still be fathers talking to their kids in 2030.
所以你们中的一个人说这实际上是世界上最乐观的节目，因为它说世界在 2030 年仍然存在，就像他们在 2030 年仍然是父亲与孩子交谈一样。

00:43:57.360 --> 00:43:58.360
Yeah.
是的。

00:43:58.360 --> 00:43:59.360
No.
不。

00:43:59.360 --> 00:44:12.440
We and we would make the joke because it is inspiring to think of and then we'd make the joke, but also like we can end the series where you realize like Ted is telling future Ted is telling his kids that story like a mile underground, the surface of the earth and I'm like, he's going to get in the shelter.
我们和我们会开这个玩笑，因为想到它很鼓舞人心，然后我们会开这个玩笑，但也就像我们可以在这个系列结束时，你意识到泰德正在告诉未来，泰德正在告诉他的孩子们这个故事，就像地下一英里，地球表面一样，我想，他会进入避难所。

00:44:12.440 --> 00:44:16.880
Serves and it's just like the machines have risen up and killed us.
发球了，就像机器起来杀死我们一样。

00:44:16.880 --> 00:44:24.440
But yeah, like that idea, there will always be future you there to tell your story.
但是，是的，就像这个想法一样，未来总会有你在那里讲述你的故事。

00:44:24.440 --> 00:44:28.159
There's a future version of you to whom this all makes more sense.
对未来版本的你来说，这一切都更有意义。

00:44:28.159 --> 00:44:35.159
And that is just so much more generous and has such gratitude for even the low moments.
这更加慷慨，即使在低谷时刻也充满感激之情。

00:44:35.159 --> 00:44:36.559
Looking back.
回头看。

00:44:36.559 --> 00:44:44.159
I think the thing you're describing is that I think open my heart the most to the series.
我认为你所描述的事情是我认为对这个系列最开放的。

00:44:44.159 --> 00:44:46.880
And I don't know how much I sense from the pilot, but I think it's all there.
我不知道我从飞行员身上感受到了多少，但我想一切都在那里。

00:44:46.880 --> 00:44:51.119
The DNA of the whole show is in the pilot, which we'll be unpacking.
整个节目的 DNA 都在试播集中，我们将对其进行解压。

00:44:51.119 --> 00:44:55.920
But it's a fundamentally optimistic show at its core.
但这本质上是一部乐观的剧。

00:44:55.920 --> 00:44:59.119
It is not a nihilistic cynical show.
这不是一场虚无主义的愤世嫉俗的表演。

00:44:59.119 --> 00:45:14.159
It's a show about decent people and even Barney who's like somewhat of a sociopathic criminal based on episode, based on, you know, whatever episode he's in, has a core of sweetness about him.
这是一部关于正派人的节目，甚至巴尼也像一个反社会的罪犯，基于剧集，基于，你知道，无论他在哪一集，他都有一个甜蜜的核心。

00:45:14.159 --> 00:45:22.440
Yeah, and the way Neil played it kind of with a wink that maybe helped him get away, you guys all get away with a lot.
是的，尼尔眨眼的方式可能帮助他逃脱，你们都逃脱了很多。

00:45:22.440 --> 00:45:30.719
But it's a fundamentally optimistic show and I just, I liked being both for professional reasons but more almost like for personal reasons.
但这从根本上来说是一个乐观的节目，我只是因为职业原因而喜欢，但更像是出于个人原因。

00:45:30.719 --> 00:45:34.440
Like I'm really conscious of what I'm putting into the collective.
就像我真的很清楚我为集体付出了什么。

00:45:34.440 --> 00:45:40.400
Like I want and you know how I made your mother made some missteps and stumbles and we're going to unpack all of those.
就像我想要的那样，你知道我是如何让你母亲犯下一些错误和绊脚石的，我们将解开所有这些。

00:45:40.400 --> 00:45:46.400
But generally I felt like it's feet were pointed in a really good direction and it's heart was in the right place.
但总的来说，我觉得它的脚指向了一个非常好的方向，它的心脏也处于正确的位置。

00:45:46.400 --> 00:45:56.400
Also how narratively invented it was, you know, there was something like 40 to 60 scenes in an average how I made my mother.
另外，它是如何叙事性地发明的，你知道，我塑造母亲的过程中平均有 40 到 60 个场景。

00:45:56.400 --> 00:45:57.400
The record was 80.
记录是80。

00:45:57.400 --> 00:45:58.400
The record was 83.
记录是83。

00:45:58.400 --> 00:46:04.280
In 21 minutes, 21.5 minutes basically you're doing 81 scenes or 60 scenes or 50 scenes like it's a lot.
在 21 分钟、21.5 分钟内，基本上你要做 81 个场景、60 个场景或 50 个场景，好像很多。

00:46:04.280 --> 00:46:05.280
Alec, you have a question.
亚历克，你有一个问题。

00:46:05.280 --> 00:46:07.719
By the way, I will say Alec Lev is chiming up a little comment.
顺便说一句，我想说亚历克·列夫正在发表一些评论。

